{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KWKuqF1TnfvvxHA8r3pjMp","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have comprehensive understanding. Let me write the architectural friction report:\n\n## Architectural Friction in Almanac Codebase\n\n**1. God Module: harden-core.sh (1977 lines)**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh`\n\nHarden-core does orchestration (fanout, ratification, fixer), ledger management, rubric validation, findings parsing, and convergence logic in one file. The deletion test fails catastrophically: removing it breaks harden entirely. The real issue: **all state mutations flow through here** — ledger appends, rubric edits, finding deduplication, status updates — yet the file doesn't export these as a cohesive ledger module. Instead, callers in `cmd/harden.sh` reach into its ledger functions directly. If you tried to plug in a different backing store (database, versioned ledger), you'd need to rewrite 40+ functions scattered across this one file with zero isolation seam.\n\n**What deletion test reveals:** No unit boundary exists around \"ledger engine\" — it's pure scripts calling awk/grep/mktemp, making it impossible to stub for testing or replace the backing store. Ratify logic (lines 845–881, `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces`) is a testable pure function buried inside orchestration.\n\n---\n\n**2. Locality Leak: run.sh's plan/slug helpers (lines 137–162)**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh`\n\n`almanac_loop_plan_dir` and `almanac_loop_slug` are \"pure\" in run.sh — they're just path joiners and slug generators with no I/O. But they're extracted into run.sh to be reused by harden-core (line 75: `almanac_loop_plan_dir harden \"$root\" \"$slug\"`) and converge-core. The real bugs hide in **call sites**: harden-core sometimes slugifies its targets on entry (line 1012: `run_id=\"$(almanac_loop_run_id \"harden\" \"$target\")\"`) and sometimes expects pre-slugified inputs (line 477: `slug=\"$(almanac_loop_slug \"$target\")\"` then uses it). This dual-contract is invisible until you trace all 91 call sites across the codebase. If a caller forgets to slugify, the path breaks silently (docs/plans/harden/Foo Bar vs Foo-bar). The extracted pure functions look modular but the responsibility for \"when to slugify\" lives scattered in harden-core, converge-core, and the skill runners.\n\n**What deletion test reveals:** These functions can't be deleted — they're needed everywhere. But moving them to a dedicated module wouldn't solve the problem: the real friction is that each loop (harden, converge, ralph) has different slugification discipline, and there's no contract enforcing it.\n\n---\n\n**3. Untested Interface: harden-core.sh and converge-core.sh (no tests)**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh`, `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh`\n\nDespite being the orchestration cores of two major loops, these have zero unit tests. `tests/test-harden-cli.sh` (93k lines) tests the CLI entry points end-to-end, but never sources harden-core.sh directly to test individual functions — no unit tests for `almanac_harden_ledger_record`, `almanac_harden_ratify`, `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces`. Converge-core has no test file at all. \n\nThe problem: **core orchestration logic is only tested through the full agent-execution path**, which requires mocked providers or real LLM calls. The pure logic (ledger deduplication at line 596–660 in harden-core, TSV parsing at line 580–591) could be unit-testable but isn't because it lives inside the orchestration file, so tests default to end-to-end mode. If the ledger dedup logic breaks, you don't find out until a full run fails.\n\n**What deletion test reveals:** Tests exist only at the CLI boundary; the internal interfaces (TSV record mutation, finding dedup, status transitions) are untested and un-stubbed, making them hard to refactor safely.\n\n---\n\n**4. Shallow Module: feedback.sh (129 lines)**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/feedback.sh`\n\nThe interface is three functions: `almanac_loop_feedback_commands` (detection), `almanac_loop_feedback_run` (execution), and `almanac_loop_feedback_markdown` (formatting). The implementation is detection (hardcoded `if [ -f package.json ]` at lines 19–52, a 34-line case-statement), then execution (`eval` at line 118), then formatting (string templates). \n\nThe complexity: each new language/tool requires editing the same detection block. The markdown generator (lines 73–88) adds a description lookup (another 12-line case-statement at lines 54–71) — a duplication. But neither is testable in isolation: `almanac_loop_feedback_run` doesn't export its verdict-emission logic, so you can't test \"what verdict does a failed npm test produce\" without spawning an actual npm test.\n\n**What deletion test reveals:** Feedback.sh is not deletable (harden and converge depend on it), but its functions are micro-modules doing one thing each with no reuse inside the file itself. The real issue: there's no \"feedback provider\" adapter pattern (unlike providers/agents). Adding a seventh language means editing feedback.sh directly, not dropping a plugin. The file *pretends* to be a cohesive module but is really a thin wrapper around `eval` with embedded tool knowledge.\n\n---\n\n**5. Cross-Seam Coupling: loops.sh / loops/<name>.sh adapter contract vs run.sh registry**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/loops.sh`, `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/loops/harden.sh`, `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh` (lines 156–164)\n\nThe loop-adapter seam (lib/loops.sh) is clean: adapters declare verbs (`launch`, `exec_argv`, `new_run_argv`, `new_run_env`, `status_to_opts`). But `status_to_opts` violates the seam boundary: **it reaches into run.sh to call `almanac_loop_status_field`** (harden.sh line 132: `almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" target`). This couples the adapter to the run-status **schema** — if you want to rename `target` to `resource`, you must edit both the record schema (run.sh line 44) AND every adapter's `status_to_opts` (harden.sh, ralph.sh, converge adapter).\n\nThe deletion test: `status_to_opts` depends on `almanac_loop_status_field` (which reads the TSV file). The field names are the real contract, not the function. If run.sh's record schema changes, adapters silently break — there's no guard function declaring \"this field must exist.\"\n\n---\n\n**6. God Module: run.sh (966 lines, but two responsibilities)**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh`\n\nRun.sh owns three tangled concerns: (1) **run-status record schema + TSV engine** (lines 36–135, the canonical field list and set/get accessors); (2) **registry control** (lines 226–270, how to spawn/track a run); (3) **hub read views** (the worker-health query functions, status-field aliases). It's not a pure god-module (each concern is roughly 200–300 lines), but they're not independently testable. \n\nThe friction: `almanac_loop_record_set` (the generic TSV engine, line 74–115) is pure and could be unit-tested standalone, but it's wrapped inside a file that also imports ui.sh and loops.sh (lines 22–34) **just to call them from hub read-views**. So if you want to test the record engine in isolation, you must test the whole file, which means sourcing the UI seam and the loop-adapter seam, which means mocking gum.\n\n**What deletion test reveals:** You can't delete run.sh because harden-core, worker.sh, and the hub all depend on it. But the record engine + registry could be split off into a separate `record.sh` + `registry.sh` pair, leaving run.sh to own only the hub read-views. The coupling to ui.sh and loops.sh (unnecessary for the record contract) is accidental.\n\n---\n\n**7. Untested Adapter Seam: loops.sh and lib/loops/*.sh (minimal test coverage)**\n**Files:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/tests/test-loops.sh`\n\n`test-loops.sh` exists (8k lines) but tests only discovery and dispatch — `almanac_loop_adapter_known`, `almanac_loop_adapter_call`. It does **not** unit-test the adapter contract verbs themselves. The `launch` verb is only tested end-to-end (via test-harden-cli, test-ralph-smoke). The `new_run_argv` / `new_run_env` composers are never unit-tested in isolation; they're tested only through the hub's resumerun path (test-hub.sh).\n\nThe friction: if a loop adapter's `exec_argv` verb is broken, you don't discover it until you launch that loop. The `new_run_opts` verb (harden.sh lines 129–146) has no unit test asserting \"given a status blob with these fields, emit this argv stream.\" The hub's resume path is the only consumer that exercises it, so refactoring the status schema requires end-to-end testing.\n\n**What deletion test reveals:** The adapter-seam functions (launch, exec_argv, new_run_argv) could each have a focused unit test without sourcing harden-core or any orchestration logic. That they don't means the seam boundary is not validated independently — it's only validated when the full loop runs.\n\n---\n\n**Summary (5–8 friction points, ~1500 words)**\n\n1. **harden-core.sh (god module, 1977 lines)** — all ledger logic in one file; deletion test fails; no ledger abstraction seam.\n2. **plan-dir/slug locality leak** — extracted pure functions but responsibility for \"when to slugify\" scattered across callers; dual contracts invisible until traced.\n3. **harden-core.sh + converge-core.sh untested** — zero unit tests; core orchestration logic only tested end-to-end; pure logic (dedup, parsing) buried inside, untestable in isolation.\n4. **feedback.sh shallow module** — three micro-functions with no isolation; tool detection hardcoded; no provider-adapter pattern; each new language requires editing one file.\n5. **loops adapter cross-seam coupling** — `status_to_opts` in adapters reaches into run.sh's record schema; renaming a field breaks multiple files.\n6. **run.sh (966 lines, mixed concerns)** — record engine + registry + hub views tangled; importing ui.sh + loops.sh for views only; record engine untestable standalone.\n7. **loops adapter seam untested** — discovery/dispatch tested, but adapter verbs (launch, new_run_argv) only tested end-to-end; no focused unit tests for the seam contract."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fd584a8-5bee-4020-89ec-8c8806d40fbd","uuid":"e863004a-84ce-485d-b2b7-182e6d56c635","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:45:21.751Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the almanac codebase at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac to surface architectural friction. The codebase is a bash-based loop engine for autonomous agents. Domain vocabulary (use exactly):\n\n- **Loop** (ralph/harden/converge) — adapter-driven\n- **Provider** (codex/claude) — adapter at `lib/providers/<name>.sh`\n- **Agent run** — `agent_capture`/`agent_stream`/`agent_raw`\n- **Run / run-status / worker-status record** — generic TSV record engine\n- **Slug** — file-safe handle\n- **Launcher** / **Hub** / **Overseer**\n- **Role** / **Lens**\n- **UI seam** — gum-or-plain\n\nWalk these directories and look for friction:\n- `lib/` — engine modules (core.sh, ui.sh, role.sh, agent.sh, run.sh, worker.sh, loop-launcher.sh, harden-core.sh, converge-core.sh, loops.sh, providers/, loops/)\n- `cmd/` — CLI entry points\n- `skills/<category>/<name>/` — skill scripts (look at converge/ralph/harden runner scripts)\n- `tests/` — what's tested vs not\n- `install.sh` and bootstrap\n\nSurface (in priority order, ~150 words each):\n1. **Shallow modules** — interface nearly as complex as implementation. Apply the deletion test.\n2. **Locality leaks** — pure functions extracted for testability but real bugs hide in call sites.\n3. *